<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 3.2 - Bases and Coordinate Systems</title>

    <introduction>
        <definition xml:id="def-">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A set of vectors in <m>\real^n</m> that spans <m>\real^n</m>
                    and is linearly independent is called a <em>basis</em> of
                    <m>\real^n</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
    </introduction>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                This question is to get you familiar with facts about bases.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the vectors <m>\vvec_1=\twovec21</m> and
                    <m>\vvec_2=\twovec12</m> form a basis for <m>\real^2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the vectors <m>\evec_1</m>, <m>\evec_2</m>, and
                    <m>\evec_3</m> form a basis for <m>\real^3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Do the vectors <m>\vvec_1=\threevec111</m>,
                    <m>\vvec_2=\threevec{0}{1}{-1}</m>,
                    <m>vvec_3 = \threevec{1}{0}{[1}</m> form a basis for
                    <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>?
                    Why or why not?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Do the vectors <m>\vvec_1=\threevec111</m>,
                    <m>\vvec_2=\threevec{0}{1}{-1}</m>,
                    <m>vvec_3 = \threevec{1}{0}{[1}</m>,
                    <m>\vvec_4=\threevec123</m> form a basis for
                    <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>?
                    Why or why not?
                    How could you do this problem without row reducing?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many vectors will be in a basis for <m>\real^{12}</m>?
                    Explain your thinking.
                    In general, how many vectors will be in a basis for
                    <m>\real^n</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\ldots,\vvec_n</m> is a
                    basis for <m>\real^n</m>.
                    Explain why every vector <m>\bvec</m> in <m>\real^n</m> can
                    be written as a linear combination of
                    <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\ldots,\vvec_n</m> in exactly one way.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace=".6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\ldots,\vvec_n</m> form a basis for
                    <m>\real^n</m> what is the row reduced echelon form of
                    <m>A = [\vvec_1\,\, \vvec_2\, \, \ldots\, \, \vvec_n]</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the invertibility of the matrix
                    <m>A</m> if its columns form a basis?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-sec3-2-basis">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A set of vectors forms a basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m> if and
                only if the matrix
                <md>
                    A = [ \vvec_1 \,\, \vvec_2 \,\, \dots \vvec_n ] \sim I.
                </md>
                This means there must be <m>m</m> vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <example>
        <p>
            Notice that the vectors
            <md>
                \evec_1 = \threevec100 , \evec_2 = \threevec010, \evec_3 = \threevec001
            </md>
            forms the columns of the <m>3\times 3</m> identity matrix which
            implies this set forms a basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
            More generally, the set of vectors
            <m>\evec_1,\evec_2,\dots,\evec_m</m> forms a basis for
            <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m> which we call the <term>standard basis</term>
            for <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
        </p>
    </example>

    <example>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>\vvec_1=\twovec21</m> and <m>\vvec_2=\twovec12</m>.
            In part 1a you explained why these vectors form a basis of
            <m>\real^2</m>, and we'll denote this basis as
            <m>\bcal=\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2\}</m>.
            Every vector in <m>\real^2</m> can be expressed in two different
            ways: in its usual form as a column vector AND as a linear
            combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
            We are going to use this to define a new coordinate system for
            <m>\real^2</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="sec3-2-fig-12basis">
            <caption>
                The grid formed by the basis vectors <m>\vvec_1=\twovec21</m>
                and <m>\vvec_2=\twovec12</m>.
            </caption>

            <image width="40%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Two vectors and a set of skewed grid lines representing
                    their linear combinations.
                </shortdescription>

                <description>
                    <p>
                        There is a set of coordinate axes and a standard
                        <m>1\times1</m> coordinate grid in the background.
                        There are also two vectors <m>\vvec_1=\twovec21</m> and
                        <m>\vvec_2=\twovec12</m> and two sets of parallel lines
                        representing the linear combinations of <m>\vvec_1</m>
                        and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                        One set of parallel lines is parallel to <m>\vvec_1</m>
                        and passes through the integer multiples of
                        <m>\vvec_2</m> while the other set is parallel to
                        <m>\vvec_2</m> and passes through integer multiples of
                        <m>\vvec_1</m>.
                    </p>
                </description>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-basis-1-2">
                    <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(250, 250)" margins="5">
                        <definition>
                            v1=(2,1)
                        </definition>

                        <definition>
                            v2=(1,2)
                        </definition>
                        <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
                            <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
                            <grid at="skew-grid" basis="(v1,v2)" cliptobbox="yes" stroke="gray"/>
                            <vector at="v1" v="v1" stroke="black">
                                <m>
                                    \vvec_1
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector at="v2" v="v2" stroke="black">
                                <m>
                                    \vvec_2
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                        </coordinates>
                        <annotations text="Two vectors and the linear combinations they create">
                            <annotation ref="v1" text="The vector v one"/>
                            <annotation ref="v2" text="The vector v two"/>
                            <annotation ref="skew-grid" text="The linear combinations they create are represented by a skewed grid of lines parallel to v one and v two"/>
                        </annotations>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            We can express the vector <m>\twovec30</m> as linear combination of
            <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>, that is
            <m>\twovec30 = 2\vvec_1 - \vvec_2</m>.
            (And we can see this using the figure above or using algebra.) In
            our new coordinate system defined by <m>\vvec_1</m> and
            <m>\vvec_2</m>, this vector has coordinates <m>2</m> and <m>-1</m>,
            that is the first coordinate in our new system is the weight of
            <m>\vvec_1</m>, and the second coordinate is the weight of
            <m>\vvec_2</m> when our vector is written as a linear combination of
            <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
            We will write this as
            <md>
                \coords{\twovec{3}0}{\bcal} = \twovec{2}{-1}.
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            In general, if <m>\xvec=c_1\vvec_1+c_2\vvec_2</m>, then
            <md>
                \coords{\xvec}{\bcal} = \twovec{c_1}{c_2}.
            </md>
            The coordinates are the weights in the linear combinations.
        </p>
    </example>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Using <xref ref="sec3-2-fig-12basis"/>, repeated below, answer
                the following questions.
            </p>

            <image width="30%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Two vectors and a set of skewed grid lines representing
                    their linear combinations.
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-basis-1-2-2">
                    <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(250, 250)" margins="5">
                        <definition>
                            v1=(2,1)
                        </definition>

                        <definition>
                            v2=(1,2)
                        </definition>
                        <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
                            <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
                            <grid at="skew-grid" basis="(v1,v2)" cliptobbox="yes" stroke="gray"/>
                            <vector at="v1" v="v1" stroke="black">
                                <m>
                                    \vvec_1
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector at="v2" v="v2" stroke="black">
                                <m>
                                    \vvec_2
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1/2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Express <m>\twovec{-4}1</m> as a linear combination of
                    <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m> to find the coordinates
                    <m>\coords{\twovec{-4}1}{\bcal}</m>.
                    Do using algebra.
                    Does your answer seem reasonable based on the figure?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1/2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the vector <m>\xvec</m> so that
                    <m>\coords{\xvec}{\bcal} =
  \twovec2{-3}</m>.
                    Can you use the figure first?
                    Then do using algebra?
                    (Notice this is ``opposite" from question (a).)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1/2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>A=\left[\vvec_1 \ \vvec_2 \right]</m>.
                    What is <m>A\twovec{-2}3</m>? Can you use the grid to determine this?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the vectors
                <md>
                    \vvec_1=\threevec10{-2},
  \vvec_2=\threevec{-2}1{0},
  \vvec_3=\threevec112
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the vectors form a basis <m>\bcal</m> for
                    <m>\real^3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the vector <m>\xvec</m> such that
                    <m>\coords{\xvec}{\bcal} =
  \threevec2{-1}{-2}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the coordinates
                    <m>\coords{\threevec{-2}{1}{-8}}{\bcal}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain how to convert <m>\{\xvec\}_\mathcal{B}</m>, the
                    representation of a vector <m>\xvec</m> in the coordinates
                    defined by <m>\mathcal{B}</m>, into <m>\xvec</m>, its
                    representation in the standard coordinate
                    system.<fn>The representation in the standard
                    coordinate system is with the standard basis vectors
                    <m>e_1,\dots, e_n</m>.</fn>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain how to convert the vector <m>\xvec</m> into
                    <m>\{\xvec\}_\mathcal{B}</m>, its representation in the
                    coordinate system defined by <m>\mathcal{B}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Read and explain <xref ref="prop-sec3-2-basis"/>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-3-2-basisconversion">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a basis and <m>P_\mathcal{B}</m> the matrix whose columns are the basis vectors, then
                <md>
                    \xvec = P_{\mathcal{B}} \{\xvec\}_{\mathcal{B}}
                </md>
                
                <md>
                    \{\xvec\}_{\mathcal{B}} = P_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}\xvec
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <outcomes>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Make sure you can answer the following questions (at least for 2
                and 3 variables):
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What is a basis?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we determine if a given set of vectors is a basis?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How many vectors are in any basis for <m>\real^n</m>?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we convert from a vector in a non-standard basis
                    <m>\mathcal{B}</m> to its standard basis coordinates
                    algebraically?
                    (That is, <m>\{\xvec\}_{\mathcal{B}}</m> to <m>\xvec</m>.)
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we convert from a vector in a standard basis to its
                    coordinates in another basis algebraically?
                    (That is, <m>\xvec</m> to <m>\{\xvec\}_{\mathcal{B}}</m>.)
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we think about non-standard bases geometrically?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>